  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Benign weather continues through the remainder \nof October with near to above normal temperatures expected. \n\nA weak cold front will stall to the south and east this\nafternoon. By Wednesday morning, the same front will retreat \nback to the north as a warm front. Another large area of high \npressure will build to the north Thursday through Saturday. As a\nresult, expect passing high clouds and a series of wind shifts \nwith each of the frontal passages. Winds will remain light out \nof the north and northwest this afternoon. Winds will change to\nthe south and southwest late Wednesday through Friday. This \nwill yield seasonable to above normal temperatures for the \nremainder of the workweek. Highs will generally range from the \nmid 60s and low 70s though Wednesday with low to mid 70s \nThursday and mid to upper 70s Friday. This is thanks to the \nposition of the high to the north leading to increased southeast\nto southwest winds across the region. Overnight lows will \nremain mild as well in the mid 40s and low 50s.\n\nThere is the potential for some low clouds, fog, or even a\nlittle drizzle during the late night into morning hours each of\nthe next few days as onshore flow develops.\n\nOtherwise, dry conditions will continue through Saturday, \nalthough this will be a day of transition as the pattern begins \nto loosen. Upper-level ridging will buckle with an incoming \ntrough from the Midwest region. This trough will ultimately \nbring an end to the dry conditions with increasing cloud cover \nand precipitation chances heading into the first few days of \nNovember. Despite the increased cloud cover, expect highs \nSaturday back in the upper 60s and low 70s. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Precipitation chances return in early November.\n\nUpper-level ridging overhead breaks down into zonal flow by \nSunday. Conditions likely start to become cloudier by the second\nhalf of the weekend as troughing develops over the Ohio River \nValley before heading east Sunday into Monday. This will bring\nthe first chances for precipitation over the Mid-Atlantic in \nover a week, although overall breadth and total rainfall from \nthis system remain uncertain at this time.\n\nTemperatures likely drop back to the low 70s over the weekend \nin the wake of another weak (dry) front. With overnight lows \ngenerally remaining in the 40s and 50s, this ranges somewhat \nabove normal for the beginning of November. Looking ahead, model\nguidance hints at a second, potentially more potent upper-level\ntrough approaching the area that could bring additional chances\nfor precipitation by the middle of next week.",
